  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1225 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nAs of 12:30 PM CDT, this morning's fog has mostly burned off and in \nits place we have mostly sunny skies. Temperatures across the area \nare in the 70s to low 80s, coolest where the fog lasted longer, with \nwinds out of the northeast to southeast. Winds west river will gust \n30-35 mph at times while winds east of the James River will \ngenerally stay below 20 mph. There is a slight chance (10-15%) for \nsome showers and weak thunderstorms late this afternoon into the \nevening hours with another round (30-55%) Saturday morning into the \nafternoon.\n\nBoth Saturday and Sunday have a marginal risk (level 1 out of 5) for \nsevere storms. Saturday, the risk covers the entire forecast area. \nSunday, the risk covers all of northeast SD and west central MN \nalong with portions of north central SD and will be mainly during \nthe afternoon and evening. There will be a pretty good cap in place \nwith 850 mb temps between 22 and 25 degrees C, but if storms can \novercome this, they may become severe. Both days the main hazards \nwill be 1 inch diameter hail and 60 mph wind gusts. Models have \nmoved the time frame for precipitation on Saturday up to include the \ndaytime hours, previously they had a pretty much dry day with storms \nstarting during the late afternoon to early evening. PoPs in general \nhave backed off on Sunday and are now less widespread, covering \nmainly areas east of the Missouri River during the afternoon and \nareas along and east of the James River during the evening. Monday \ndoes not have an outlook yet, but there is a good chance there will \nbe one as low pressure enters the region and has lots of Gulf \nmoisture to work with. PWATs are still in the 99.5 NAEFS percentile \nor above for most of Monday with values between 1.5 and 1.75 inches. \nThis will help storms intensify and could also lead to some heavy \ndownpours during storms. These storm chances look to continue into \nearly Tuesday morning.\n\nHighs Saturday are still expected to be above average by as much as \n20 degrees then temperatures will cool down into next work week and \nbe much closer to average. After today, winds look to be pretty \nnormal until Monday when gusts of 30-35 mph will be possible across \nmost of the forecast area during the afternoon, lingering over \nnortheast SD during the evening and potentially into Tuesday morning.",
